      "problem": "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe integer $17$ \n{{field-1}}\nthe additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ since $17+12$ {{field-2}} $(\\text{mod}\\,29).$",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ is the integer $0\\le x<29$ such that\n\n$$\nx+12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,29).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $29:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17+12 & ≡29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,29) \\\\  & ≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $17$ $\\text{is}$ the additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ since $17+12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,29).$",
